# Linux Zero to Hero — Sections 3, 4 & 5: User Management, File Management & Vi Shortcuts

---

## Section 3 — User Management

### Why User Management?

On a personal laptop, a single admin user is fine — accountability is clear. But on a **production Linux server** shared by an entire organization (developers, QA, DevOps, management), sharing the `root` user across everyone creates serious problems:

- If a developer accidentally deletes `/sbin` (system binaries), the server is corrupted.
- With a shared root account, **there is no way to trace who did it** — this is called **lack of accountability**.
- There is no way to restrict what each person can or cannot do.

**Solution:** Create individual user accounts and groups, and grant permissions based on roles.

| Role | Typical Access |
|---|---|
| DevOps Engineer | Admin / elevated privileges |
| Developer | Read/write to app dirs; no access to `/sbin` |
| Manager | Read-only access |
| QA Engineer | Access to logs and test environments |

---

### Creating Users

#### `useradd` — Quick User Creation

```bash
# Create a user (no home directory, no prompts)
useradd avirala

# Verify the user was created
cat /etc/passwd
```

The `/etc/passwd` file stores all user account information. An entry in this file confirms the user exists.

#### `adduser` — Interactive User Creation (Recommended)

```bash
# Create a user with home directory and full details
adduser avirala
```

`adduser` prompts for:
- Password
- Full name
- Room number, work phone, home phone (optional — can be skipped)

It also **automatically creates a home directory** at `/home/avirala`.

```bash
# Verify home directory was created
ls /home
```

---

### Setting & Managing Passwords

```bash
# Set or change a user's password (run as root/admin)
passwd avirala
```

The encrypted password is stored in `/etc/shadow`:

```bash
cat /etc/shadow
```

> **Important:** Passwords in `/etc/shadow` use **one-way SHA-256 hashing**. Decryption is **not possible**. If a user forgets their password, the old password **cannot be restored** — a new one must be set.

#### Password Expiry Policy

```bash
# Force password change every 90 days
chage -M 90 avirala
```

---

### Deleting Users

```bash
# Delete a user
userdel avirala

# Confirm the user no longer exists
cat /etc/passwd
```

---

### `useradd` vs `adduser` — Key Difference

| Feature | `useradd` | `adduser` |
|---|---|---|
| Home directory created | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |
| Prompts for user details | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |
| Best used for | Shell scripts / automation | Interactive user creation |

> **Why use `useradd` at all?** In **shell scripts**, you need non-interactive commands. `useradd` creates a user and moves immediately to the next line — no prompts, no pauses. `adduser` is interactive and unsuitable for scripting.

---

### Locking & Unlocking User Accounts

```bash
# Lock a user account (e.g., suspicious activity)
usermod -L avirala

# Unlock the user account
usermod -U avirala
```

Use locking when an account needs to be temporarily suspended without deleting it.

---

### Group Management

#### Why Groups?

Managing permissions for 500+ individual users is impractical. Instead, users are placed in **groups**, and permissions are applied at the group level. When a permission changes, updating the group automatically affects all members.

```
Organization (500 users)
├── dev     (250 developers)
├── qa      (200 QA engineers)
├── devops  (30 DevOps engineers)
└── mgmt    (10 managers)
```

#### Creating a Group

```bash
groupadd devops

# Verify the group was created
cat /etc/group
```

#### Adding a User to a Group

```bash
# usermod -aG <groupname> <username>
usermod -aG devops avirala

# Verify user is in the group
cat /etc/group
```

The `-aG` flags mean: `-a` (append, don't remove from other groups) and `-G` (specify group name).

#### Key Group Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/etc/group` | Lists all groups and their members |
| `/etc/passwd` | Lists all users |
| `/etc/shadow` | Stores encrypted user passwords |

---

### Connecting to a Remote Linux Server via SSH

In a real organization, a Linux administrator creates a user on the server and shares the IP address, username, and password. You then connect using **SSH**.

#### How SSH Works

- Every Linux server runs a background process called **`sshd`** (SSH daemon) that listens for incoming connections.
- On your local machine (Windows/Mac), you use an **SSH client** to connect.
- **Windows:** Git Bash or WSL
- **Mac:** Terminal (built-in) or iTerm2

#### Connecting via SSH

```bash
# ssh <username>@<server-ip>
ssh avirala@172.31.87.187
# Enter the password when prompted
```

```bash
# Confirm who you are after logging in
whoami

# Check system details
uname -a
```

#### Enabling / Disabling Password Authentication

Cloud providers often **disable password authentication by default** for security. To enable it:

```bash
# Edit the SSH config file
vim /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/<config-file>

# Change this line:
PasswordAuthentication yes   # (set to 'no' to disable)
```

```bash
# Restart the SSH service to apply changes
systemctl restart ssh
```

> If password authentication is disabled, login is done using **PEM key files** (SSH key pairs) — covered in a later section.

---

## Section 4 — File Management

### Navigating the File System

```bash
# List files and directories in current location
ls

# List with details (permissions, size, date)
ls -ltr

# Change into a directory
cd /tmp

# Go up one level (parent directory)
cd ..

# Go up two levels
cd ../..

# Go to the root of the file system
cd /

# Show current working directory
pwd
```

---

### Creating Files & Directories

```bash
# Create an empty file
touch abfile

# Create a directory
mkdir avirala

# Verify
ls -ltr
```

> In `ls -ltr` output, entries starting with `d` are directories; others are files.

---

### Deleting Files & Directories

```bash
# Delete a file
rm abfile

# Delete an empty directory
rmdir avirala

# Delete a directory and all its contents (force recursive)
rm -rf avirala
```

> ⚠️ `rm -rf` is irreversible. Always double-check the path before running it.

---

### Copying & Renaming Files

```bash
# Copy a file (creates a duplicate with a new name)
cp ab viraala

# Rename / move a file
mv ab viraala

# Move a file to a different directory
mv abfile /tmp/
```

---

### Reading File Contents

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `cat filename` | Print entire file content to terminal |
| `less filename` | Open file in scrollable interactive view (press `q` to quit) |
| `head -n 10 filename` | Print the first 10 lines |
| `tail -n 20 filename` | Print the last 20 lines |
| `tac filename` | Print file in reverse order (novelty, rarely used) |

```bash
# Install less if not available
apt install less

# Examples
cat demofile.txt
less demofile.txt
head -10 demofile.txt
tail -20 demofile.txt
```

---

### Writing to Files with `echo`

`echo` is the print statement of the shell. Combined with redirection operators, it writes to files.

```bash
# Overwrite a file with new content (ALL previous content is deleted)
echo "Hello World" > demofile.txt

# Append content to an existing file (preserves existing content)
echo "Hello" >> demofile.txt
echo "World" >> demofile.txt

# View result
cat demofile.txt
```

> **Critical distinction:**
> - `>` — **overwrites** the file completely
> - `>>` — **appends** to the file without deleting existing content

---

## Section 5 — Vi / Vim Editor & Shortcuts

Vim (Vi Improved) is the standard text editor for Linux. It is used to create and edit files directly on the server — no GUI required.

```bash
# Install vim
apt install vim

# Open an existing file or create a new one
vim abfile
```

---

### The Three Modes of Vim

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    VIM MODES                         │
│                                                      │
│  NORMAL MODE       INSERT MODE       COMMAND MODE    │
│  (default)    →i→  (write/edit)  →ESC→  (:commands) │
│  navigation        type content       save / quit    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

| Mode | How to Enter | What You Can Do |
|---|---|---|
| **Normal Mode** | Default when file opens / press `Esc` | Navigate — scroll up, down, left, right |
| **Insert Mode** | Press `i` in Normal Mode | Type and edit content (see `-- INSERT --` at bottom) |
| **Command Mode** | Press `Esc` then `:` | Run save/quit/search instructions |

---

### Opening and Writing to a File

1. Open a file with `vim abfile`
2. Press `i` to enter **Insert Mode** — you will see `-- INSERT --` at the bottom
3. Type your content freely
4. Press `Esc` to exit Insert Mode and return to Normal Mode

---

### Saving and Quitting

All save/quit commands are entered in **Command Mode** (press `Esc` then `:`):

| Command | Action |
|---|---|
| `:wq!` | Save and quit |
| `:w` | Save without quitting |
| `:q!` | Quit without saving (discard changes) |

```
# Workflow:
vim abfile        → opens the file
i                 → enter Insert Mode
(type content)
Esc               → return to Normal Mode
:wq!              → save and quit
```

```bash
# Verify saved content
cat abfile
```

---

### Navigation Shortcuts

These shortcuts work in **Normal Mode** (press `Esc` first):

| Shortcut | Action |
|---|---|
| Arrow keys (`↑` `↓` `←` `→`) | Move cursor line by line / character by character |
| `:0` | Jump to the **first line** of the file |
| `:<n>` | Jump to **line number `n`** (e.g., `:500` goes to line 500) |
| `Shift + G` | Jump to the **last line** of the file |
| `Shift + O` | Insert a **new line above** the cursor |
| `u` | **Undo** the last change |
| `/searchterm` | **Search** for a word in the file (press `n` for next match) |

```bash
# Example: open a 1000-line file and jump to line 500
vim demofile.txt
# Press Esc, then type:
:500
```

> For a complete reference of all Vim shortcuts (insert, navigation, search & replace), refer to the course GitHub repository `vi-shortcuts.md`.

---

## Important Files Reference

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/etc/passwd` | All user accounts on the system |
| `/etc/shadow` | Encrypted passwords for all users |
| `/etc/group` | All groups and their members |
| `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` | SSH server configuration (enable/disable password auth) |

---

## Interview Questions to Remember

**Q1: What is the difference between `useradd` and `adduser`?**

`useradd` is a low-level command that creates a user quickly without prompts and **does not create a home directory**. It is preferred in **shell scripts** for non-interactive automation. `adduser` is a higher-level command that prompts for user details and **automatically creates a home directory**. It is preferred for manual, interactive user creation.

---

**Q2: Can you restore a forgotten Linux user password?**

**No.** Passwords are stored in `/etc/shadow` as a **one-way SHA-256 hash**. Hashing is irreversible — decryption is not possible. If a user forgets their password, the administrator must **set a new password** using `passwd <username>`. The original password cannot be recovered.

---

**Q3: Why are groups used in Linux?**

Groups allow administrators to manage permissions for large numbers of users efficiently. Instead of modifying permissions for each of 250 developers individually, a single group permission change applies to all members simultaneously. Groups make access control scalable.

---

## Section Summary

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `useradd <name>` | Create user (no home dir, no prompts — for scripts) |
| `adduser <name>` | Create user with home dir and details (interactive) |
| `passwd <name>` | Set or change a user's password |
| `userdel <name>` | Delete a user |
| `usermod -L <name>` | Lock a user account |
| `usermod -U <name>` | Unlock a user account |
| `chage -M 90 <name>` | Set password expiry to 90 days |
| `groupadd <name>` | Create a new group |
| `usermod -aG <group> <user>` | Add a user to a group |
| `ssh <user>@<ip>` | Connect to a remote Linux server |
| `ls` / `ls -ltr` | List files and directories |
| `cd <dir>` | Change directory |
| `pwd` | Print current working directory |
| `mkdir <name>` | Create a directory |
| `touch <name>` | Create an empty file |
| `rm <file>` | Delete a file |
| `rmdir <dir>` | Delete an empty directory |
| `rm -rf <dir>` | Force delete directory and all contents |
| `cp <src> <dst>` | Copy a file |
| `mv <src> <dst>` | Move or rename a file |
| `cat <file>` | Print file contents to terminal |
| `less <file>` | View file in scrollable mode |
| `head -n <file>` | Print first n lines |
| `tail -n <file>` | Print last n lines |
| `echo "text" > file` | Overwrite file with text |
| `echo "text" >> file` | Append text to file |
| `vim <file>` | Open file in Vim editor |
